--TEST--
`executeMany` takes one `$params` array per execution and `execute`'s own flat array is not one of them, so nothing has to guess how a batch was meant to be split — `rule:core-classes/db-statement-members` and `rule:core-classes/db-parameters`, spec § 18
--FILE--
<?nvs
var $db = Core\Db::connect("main");

// One set, which is `execute`'s `$params` with an array around it. The
// statement is written once and bound once per set, so the sets are the only
// thing that varies and the SQL cannot pick up a fragment per execution.
uint $one = $db->executeMany("insert into accounts (id, owner) values (?, ?)", [[1, "ada"]]);

// The other end of the same shape: no sets at all is a batch with nothing to
// do, which is § 4's no-op and not a missing argument.
uint $none = $db->executeMany("insert into accounts (id, owner) values (?, ?)", []);

// And the refusal. This is `execute`'s second argument passed unchanged, and a
// member that accepted it would have to choose between reading it as one set
// and as a list of one-value sets — both of which write something. The refusal
// lands on each element rather than on the array, which is the diagnostic that
// says what the outer array is *for*.
uint $flat = $db->executeMany("insert into accounts (id, owner) values (?, ?)", [1, "ada"]);
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `array<mixed>`, found `int`
   --> case.nvs:18:82
%A
error: aborting due to 2 errors
